WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.480
- So Chuck, what's been
bugging you lately?

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:05.050
- Dorian, Maria, Irma...

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:06.000
- Your ex-girlfriends?

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.063
- No.
(laughter)

00:00:07.063 --> 00:00:09.646
(smooth music)

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:12.667
- Hurricanes, I get it, I get it.

00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:14.150
- How they form is brilliant.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:14.983
- You know how they form?

00:00:14.983 --> 00:00:15.990
- Yeah.
- Okay, okay.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:17.290
Yeah, I don't know if people know

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:20.120
that each of these low pressure systems

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.600
that build up in the South Atlantic,

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.620
they each get named alphabetically,

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:28.900
alternating boy name, girl name,

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:30.520
so that's how you know how deep into

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:33.300
the hurricane season you
are, the storm season,

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.310
by how far down the alphabet you've gone.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:36.143
- [Chuck] Wow.

00:00:36.143 --> 00:00:39.720
- And so do you think people
know how they're formed?

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.050
I can--
- No, go ahead.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:42.250
- Okay, I need an Earth.

00:00:44.569 --> 00:00:46.049
There it goes.
- Oh, well look at that.

00:00:46.049 --> 00:00:46.882
(laughs)

00:00:46.882 --> 00:00:47.940
Any planet you want is here.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:50.160
- Yeah, any of the eight planets.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:50.993
Get over it.

00:00:50.993 --> 00:00:52.100
Okay.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:54.140
So we all know Earth rotates.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:54.973
- Right.

00:00:54.973 --> 00:00:57.050
- And on the equator, they actually rotate

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:58.920
faster than anybody else.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.710
Their speed through space is faster.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:02.543
- Makes sense.

00:01:02.543 --> 00:01:05.380
- 'Cause they make a day's
trip like everybody else does,

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:07.600
but they've gone 25,000 miles,

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.370
and anyone else has gone--
- Less.

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:10.620
- Less, all right.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:12.050
That's fact number one.

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:15.230
Fact number two, let's
throw a low pressure system

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:17.770
in the Atlantic, and you're a puffy cloud.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:18.730
Which way you gonna go?

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:20.040
- You're gonna push me north.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.170
- Yeah, so all the system
will push you north

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:23.730
towards that low pressure system.

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:26.250
And I'm another puffy cloud north

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:27.760
of the low pressure system.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:28.593
Which way are you gonna go?

00:01:28.593 --> 00:01:29.426
- I'm gonna go south.

00:01:29.426 --> 00:01:30.259
- You're gonna go south.

00:01:30.259 --> 00:01:33.280
So the low pressure system
attracts all clouds.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:34.280
- Right, oh wow.

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.150
- Calling all the
clouds, so there they go.

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:38.750
Now, if that kept up,
they would just sorta

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:40.329
meet in the middle, but
that's not what happens.

00:01:40.329 --> 00:01:41.162
- Hey, what's up?

00:01:41.162 --> 00:01:41.995
How you doing?

00:01:41.995 --> 00:01:44.140
- That's not what happens because

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:46.250
everybody has sideways motion.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:49.800
And I'm a cloud south of
that low pressure system,

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.430
and I go towards it, but wait a minute.

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:53.300
I have speed that will overtake it,

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:56.030
'cause I'm going sideways
faster than it is.

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:56.863
- [Chuck] Okay.

00:01:56.863 --> 00:01:57.710
- [Neil] It'll overtake it to the right.

00:01:57.710 --> 00:01:58.543
- Right.

00:01:58.543 --> 00:02:00.760
- Now you're above, and
you're headed towards it.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.672
You're gonna fall behind it to the right.

00:02:03.672 --> 00:02:04.505
- Oh.

00:02:04.505 --> 00:02:07.590
- And that creates a general circulation

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:10.050
of all weather around
a low pressure system,

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:11.290
and that's what we call a storm.

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:12.680
And so Chuck, it probably makes sense

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.010
that if you repeat this in
the Southern Hemisphere,

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:17.270
everything is gonna move
in the opposite direction.

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:18.840
So storms in the Southern Hemisphere

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:21.930
actually rotate clockwise,
and that would mean

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:24.883
a storm on the equator wouldn't
know which way to rotate.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:27.550
- You've got a storm-free zone.

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:28.590
- It's a storm-free zone.

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:29.470
- [Chuck] Nice.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:30.410
- All right, and there you go.

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:33.210
So Chuck, the fact that
things circulate in this way

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:35.400
with clouds from below overtaking

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.790
and from above falling
behind, that was studied

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:40.080
by a guy named Coriolis, and it's called

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:41.160
the Coriolis force.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:42.227
- Aah.

00:02:42.227 --> 00:02:44.260
- So it's not really a
force, but it looks like

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:45.870
there's a force pushing things,

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:48.490
and that's what happens when
you're on rotating surfaces,

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:50.900
and not only that, how do you get a cloud

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.020
in the first place?

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:55.220
You need moist air that's unstable,

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:56.410
which means it'll rise.

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:57.243
- Right.

00:02:57.243 --> 00:02:58.610
- So if you have moist, rising air,

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:00.893
it's colder up there than down here.

00:03:00.893 --> 00:03:01.810
You know that if you've
been in an airplane

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:03.290
and look at the temperature
outside the plane.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:04.320
- Not very pleasant.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.310
- Not much, it's like 40 below zero,

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:07.830
'cause all the sunlight passes through

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:10.447
our transparent atmosphere,
hits the ground,

00:03:10.447 --> 00:03:11.750
and the ground heats the air.

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:13.020
- Right.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:14.670
- The sun doesn't heat the atmosphere,

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:16.820
except in some very special layers of it.

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:19.300
The sun heats the ground,
the ground heats the air.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:21.930
- So the radiation of heat back--

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:22.840
- Back!
- Is where the energy's

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:23.673
coming from.

00:03:23.673 --> 00:03:24.559
- Correct.

00:03:24.559 --> 00:03:25.520
That's what we feel, because that becomes

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:26.750
that back in infrared.

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:27.610
That's how that works.

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:28.644
- [Chuck] Cool.

00:03:28.644 --> 00:03:30.960
- Comes in as visible light,
gets re-radiated as infrared.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:31.793
- [Chuck] Okay.

00:03:31.793 --> 00:03:34.230
- Yeah, so, what happens?

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:36.450
So the ground absorbs
it and it re-radiates it

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:37.650
later in the day.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.760
So the hottest time of day
is not at peak heating.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:42.551
It's a little later when
the ground gives it back.

00:03:42.551 --> 00:03:43.384
- [Chuck] Three o'clock.

00:03:43.384 --> 00:03:44.800
- Two or three o'clock in the afternoon.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:48.400
The hottest month of the year is not June,

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:49.880
which has the first day of summer,

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:50.850
the longest day of the year, and that has

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:51.683
the most ground heat.

00:03:51.683 --> 00:03:52.520
No.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:54.780
- Like August.
- Because there's a delay,

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:57.772
the oceans pick up this energy, okay?

00:03:57.772 --> 00:04:01.163
This thermal energy, and
it gives it off later.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:03.910
Later, so wait, when is hurricane season?

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:04.820
- August.

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:07.450
- It's August and September, okay?

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:09.340
Because Earth is beginning to cool down,

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:11.030
but you have this heat source.

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:12.470
- That's still--
- That's still, and it's

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:15.070
a moisture source, and it's unstable.

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:15.903
- [Chuck] Wow.

00:04:15.903 --> 00:04:18.020
- So the air rises, makes clouds,

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:20.410
low pressure system gathers, you have

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:23.202
a circulating system,
thus is born a hurricane.

00:04:23.202 --> 00:04:24.600
- Hurricane, boom!

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:25.620
- Boom, boom!

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:26.453
- Look at that.

00:04:26.453 --> 00:04:30.380
- Now, hurricanes, the lower
the low pressure system is,

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:31.960
the more clouds it attracted, the more

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:33.080
devastating the hurricane is.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:34.510
The faster it'll turn, everything.

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:35.343
- Look at that.

00:04:35.343 --> 00:04:36.440
- Yeah, so that's that.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:39.440
Now, as it sorta, they can
sometimes make a left turn

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:43.010
into the Caribbean, as
it so devastatingly does

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:44.590
for the island countries there,

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:45.570
or it can go up the coast.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:46.403
- Right.

00:04:46.403 --> 00:04:48.690
- All right, now, if it goes over land,

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:50.890
doesn't have the heat from the ocean,

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:53.020
and so generally they lose their punch.

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:54.240
- Less heat, less moisture.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:55.950
- Exactly, generally
they lose their punch,

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:58.310
but if it stays along the
coast, it can stay potent.

00:04:58.310 --> 00:04:59.143
- Aha.

00:04:59.143 --> 00:05:00.760
- And they don't move very fast.

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:02.720
I mean, along Earth's surface, 10 miles,

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:03.830
20 miles an hour tops.

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:04.663
- Right.

00:05:04.663 --> 00:05:05.496
- Okay.

00:05:05.496 --> 00:05:07.580
That means if you do the math for how long

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:08.880
it would take it to go up the coast,

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:10.810
it can take weeks.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:11.770
Guaranteeing that--

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:13.237
- But I'm a hurricane,
I got no place to be.

00:05:13.237 --> 00:05:14.480
- I got time. (laughs)

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:15.380
- I got time, baby.

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:19.760
- Some place along this
coast, it's gonna landfall

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:21.240
during high tide.

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:22.860
Somebody's gonna get
a high tide hurricane.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:23.693
- Right.

00:05:23.693 --> 00:05:26.710
- And that's bad, because high tide

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:28.980
can be bad enough, but a high tide

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:31.440
that would be during full
moon or during new moon,

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:32.440
that's what I mean by high tide.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:33.670
Every day has a high tide.

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:36.390
I'm talking about big high tide, right?

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:37.590
Full moon, new moon.

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:39.680
The hurricane hits you
during one of those,

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:42.830
you get extra tidal, you get storm surge

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:44.240
as well as the tide surge.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:45.073
- [Chuck] Wow.

00:05:45.073 --> 00:05:47.310
- And so that can, particularly in the era

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:51.490
of global warming where ocean levels

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:54.670
are slowly creeping up,
you can end up going over

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:57.920
gates and jetties and levies or whatever

00:05:57.920 --> 00:05:59.000
is holding back the water.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:00.470
You can breach that.

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:02.953
Just takes an inch to breach it.

00:06:02.953 --> 00:06:04.553
- That's it, flooded the city.

00:06:04.553 --> 00:06:05.837
- You can flood the
city, 'cause as long as

00:06:05.837 --> 00:06:07.470
the hurricane is there,
you flood the city.

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:08.303
- Get your buckets out.

00:06:08.303 --> 00:06:10.300
- But that's what happened
in Hurricane Sandy,

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:11.133
here in New York.

00:06:11.133 --> 00:06:13.170
I lost electricity downtown, 'cause they

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:14.330
went over the side wall.

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:15.200
- That's all it was, yeah.

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:18.460
And then the storm surge
was not even a foot.

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:19.700
- But it just has to be
whatever it is above.

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:21.380
- [Chuck] Whatever it is above, yeah.

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:23.090
- That's all you needed above the limit.

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:23.960
Yeah, you got it.

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:25.160
Whole underground parking lots,

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:27.550
cars were bobbing out of the parking lot.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:29.830
So here we are in hurricane season.

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:30.730
That's what's going on.

00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:31.563
- There you go.

00:06:31.563 --> 00:06:34.060
Everything you wanted to know
about hurricanes and more,

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:35.060
right there.

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:35.893
- Mm.

